Pass by Value in Java
    Pass by value means Java passes a copy of a variable's value to a method.
    When you pass a variable to a method, the original variable is not directly
    passed. The method receives a copy.

Example 1: Passing a primitive value
    static void changeValue(int x) {
            x = 50;
            System.out.println("Inside method: " + x);
        }

        public static void main(String[] args) {

            int num = 10;

            changeValue(num);

            System.out.println("Outside method: " + num);
        }

Why?
1. num contains 10.
2. changeValue(num) passes a copy of num.
3. Inside the method, x becomes 50.
4. The original num remains 10.

 2. Varargs in Java
    Varargs (Variable Arguments) allow a method to accept a variable number of arguments.

    Normally, a method requires a fixed number of arguments. With varargs, you can pass zero, one, or multiple arguments.

    Syntax
            returnType methodName(dataType... variableName) {
                // code
            }

    example -
                 static int sum(int... numbers) {

                        int total = 0;

                        for (int num : numbers) {
                            total = total + num;
                        }

                        return total;
                    }

                    public static void main(String[] args) {

                        System.out.println(sum(10, 20));
                        System.out.println(sum(10, 20, 30));
                        System.out.println(sum(10, 20, 30, 40));
                    }

    How does varargs work?
    - int... numbers accepts multiple integer arguments.
    - Inside the method, numbers behaves like an array.
    - You can use loops to access its elements.
    - You can pass zero or more arguments.

 Important rules of varargs
    Rule 1: A method can have only one varargs parameter.
            static void display(int... a) {
            }
    Rule 2: Varargs must be the last parameter.
            static void display(String name, int... marks) {
            }
    Rule 3: You can combine normal parameters with varargs.
            static void student(String name, int... marks) {

                System.out.println(name);

                for (int mark : marks) {
                    System.out.println(mark);
                }
            }

            Call - student("Aman", 80, 85, 90);

Recursion in Java
    Recursion is a technique in which a method calls itself to solve a problem.
    A recursive method keeps calling itself with a smaller or simpler input until it reaches a stopping condition.

    Every recursive method should have two important parts:
        1. Base condition: Stops the recursion.
        2. Recursive call: Calls the same method again.

            static void printNumber(int n) {

                // Base condition
                if (n > 5) {
                    return;
                }

                System.out.println(n);

                // Recursive call
                printNumber(n + 1);
            }

            public static void main(String[] args) {

                printNumber(1);
            }

Factorial
            static int factorial(int n) {

                if (n == 0 || n == 1) {
                    return 1;
                }

                return n * factorial(n - 1);
            }

            public static void main(String[] args) {

                int result = factorial(5);

                System.out.println(result);
            }